# Line endings are decided here, not by each machine's core.autocrlf.
#
# smugglr#382. A test in smugglr-forger read its own source to locate pasted
# blocks and could not find them on Windows, because git checked the file out
# CRLF there and the search needle ended in a bare newline. `main` was red on
# one platform for three merges. That defect was fixed on its own terms in
# smugglr#380 -- the locating is line-ending agnostic now, and its guard
# constructs a CRLF copy in memory so the failure is reachable on every
# platform. This file is the other half: it removes the state rather than
# testing for it.
#
# Measured before adding: `git ls-files --eol` reported 188 files stored `i/lf`
# and 2 stored `i/-text`, and zero stored CRLF or mixed. So this normalizes
# nothing and produces no renormalization diff -- the blast radius that makes a
# line-ending policy risky to introduce is, here, empty.

# Everything git considers text is stored LF and checked out LF, on every
# platform. `eol=lf` is the load-bearing half: `text=auto` alone would still
# check out CRLF on Windows under the default `core.autocrlf`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries, named rather than left to detection. git already infers these two
# (they are the `i/-text` pair above), and saying so means a future image
# cannot be corrupted by a heuristic that guesses wrong on a file that happens
# to contain no NUL byte early on.
*.webp binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
